libdatachannel 0.24.6's DtlsTransport::start() (the OpenSSL backend) registers its incoming
callback, which replays at once onto the thread pool the records the ICE transport kept while no
DTLS transport listened, before it sets the DTLS MTU under the SSL mutex. When the peer's
ClientHello arrived before this side's ICE transport connected, doRecv can take the mutex first
and answer it while the MTU is still unset: with SSL_OP_NO_QUERY_MTU, OpenSSL's dtls1_query_mtu
fails the write without an error on its queue, SSL_get_error gives SSL_ERROR_SYSCALL, and the
handshake fails ("DTLS recv: Handshake failed: fatal I/O error"), so the connection goes to
failed. webcpp::webrtc's native tests met it about once in 300 runs under load (the native
run's a_full_exchange on MSVC 14.3, 2026-11-23). This patch sets the MTU before the callback is
registered. Remove this file once the libdatachannel that tools/libdatachannel/pin.json pins
sets the MTU first: build.py then fails, saying so.

This patch changes files of libdatachannel, which the Mozilla Public License 2.0 covers, and is
itself under that licence.

diff --git a/src/impl/dtlstransport.cpp b/src/impl/dtlstransport.cpp
--- a/src/impl/dtlstransport.cpp
+++ b/src/impl/dtlstransport.cpp
@@ -856,16 +856,22 @@ DtlsTransport::~DtlsTransport() {
 
 void DtlsTransport::start() {
 	PLOG_DEBUG << "Starting DTLS transport";
-	registerIncoming();
-	changeState(State::Connecting);
-
-	int ret, err;
 	{
+		// The MTU is set before any incoming record can reach doRecv: registerIncoming() replays
+		// the records the ICE transport kept, and a peer's ClientHello among them would otherwise
+		// make the handshake write before the MTU exists, which fails it.
 		std::lock_guard lock(mSslMutex);
 
 		size_t mtu = mMtu.value_or(DEFAULT_MTU) - 8 - 40; // UDP/IPv6
 		SSL_set_mtu(mSsl, static_cast<unsigned int>(mtu));
 		PLOG_VERBOSE << "DTLS MTU set to " << mtu;
+	}
+	registerIncoming();
+	changeState(State::Connecting);
+
+	int ret, err;
+	{
+		std::lock_guard lock(mSslMutex);
 
 		// Initiate the handshake
 		ret = SSL_do_handshake(mSsl);
